# Deployment

## Pipelines

Two GitHub Actions workflows run on every push to `main`, on every pull request and on demand.

**CI** (`.github/workflows/ci.yml`) needs no credentials:

| Job | Checks |
| --- | --- |
| Terraform | {% if grpc %}Compiles `${{ values.grpcSpec }}` with `protoc`, then `terraform fmt -check`{% else %}`terraform fmt -check`{% endif %}, `terraform init -backend=false`, `terraform validate`, `terraform test`, `tflint` |
| Security scan | Trivy scan of `infra/`; fails on HIGH and CRITICAL findings |
{%- if not grpc %}
| API definition | `scripts/validate_openapi.py`: `${{ values.openApiSpec }}` is valid OpenAPI 2.0 and every operation has an `operationId` |
{%- endif %}
| Documentation | `mkdocs build --strict` with the TechDocs plugins |

**Deploy** (`.github/workflows/deploy.yml`):

| Job | Runs | Does |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Deployment credentials | always | Checks whether the three settings below exist |
| Plan | when they exist | {% if grpc %}Compiles the descriptor, then `terraform plan`{% else %}`terraform plan`{% endif %}; the plan is kept as an artifact |
| Apply | after Plan, on `main` only | `terraform apply` of that plan, in the `production` environment |

Until the settings exist, Plan and Apply are skipped and the workflow ends successfully. Nothing is deployed by creating the repository.

## One-time setup

The commands use the project `${{ values.projectId }}` and the repository `${{ repo }}`.

### 1. Enable the bootstrap APIs

Terraform enables the Endpoints and Cloud Run APIs itself, but it needs these to do so:

```bash
gcloud services enable --project ${{ values.projectId }} \
  cloudresourcemanager.googleapis.com serviceusage.googleapis.com \
  iam.googleapis.com iamcredentials.googleapis.com sts.googleapis.com
```

### 2. Create the state bucket

```bash
gcloud storage buckets create gs://${{ values.projectId }}-tfstate \
  --project ${{ values.projectId }} --location europe-west1 \
  --uniform-bucket-level-access
gcloud storage buckets update gs://${{ values.projectId }}-tfstate --versioning
```

Any bucket works; the state is stored under the prefix `${{ values.destination.repo }}`.

### 3. Create the deployment identity

GitHub Actions signs in with Workload Identity Federation: no key is stored anywhere.

```bash
PROJECT_ID=${{ values.projectId }}
PROJECT_NUMBER=$(gcloud projects describe "$PROJECT_ID" --format 'value(projectNumber)')
DEPLOYER="terraform-deployer@$PROJECT_ID.iam.gserviceaccount.com"

gcloud iam service-accounts create terraform-deployer --project "$PROJECT_ID"

gcloud iam workload-identity-pools create github \
  --project "$PROJECT_ID" --location global
gcloud iam workload-identity-pools providers create-oidc github \
  --project "$PROJECT_ID" --location global --workload-identity-pool github \
  --issuer-uri https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com \
  --attribute-mapping "google.subject=assertion.sub,attribute.repository=assertion.repository" \
  --attribute-condition "assertion.repository == '${{ repo }}'"

gcloud iam service-accounts add-iam-policy-binding "$DEPLOYER" \
  --project "$PROJECT_ID" --role roles/iam.workloadIdentityUser \
  --member "principalSet://iam.googleapis.com/projects/$PROJECT_NUMBER/locations/global/workloadIdentityPools/github/attribute.repository/${{ repo }}"
```

Grant the deployer these roles on the project (`gcloud projects add-iam-policy-binding "$PROJECT_ID" --member "serviceAccount:$DEPLOYER" --role <role>`):

| Role | Needed for |
| --- | --- |
| `roles/servicemanagement.admin` | The Endpoints service, its configurations and its IAM policy |
| `roles/serviceusage.serviceUsageAdmin` | Enabling APIs |
| `roles/run.admin` | The gateway on Cloud Run |
| `roles/iam.serviceAccountAdmin` | The service account of the gateway |
| `roles/iam.serviceAccountUser` | Deploying the gateway with that service account |
| `roles/resourcemanager.projectIamAdmin` | Granting the gateway's service account its roles |
{%- if values.enableMonitoring %}
| `roles/monitoring.editor` | Dashboard, alert policies, notification channels |
{%- endif %}
{%- if values.enableLogging %}
| `roles/logging.configWriter` | Log bucket and sink |
{%- endif %}

and `roles/storage.objectAdmin` on the state bucket.

### 4. Tell the repository

Under **Settings > Secrets and variables > Actions** of the repository add three variables (the first two may be secrets instead):

| Name | Value |
| --- | --- |
| `GCP_WORKLOAD_IDENTITY_PROVIDER` | `projects/<project number>/locations/global/workloadIdentityPools/github/providers/github` |
| `GCP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT` | `terraform-deployer@${{ values.projectId }}.iam.gserviceaccount.com` |
| `TF_STATE_BUCKET` | `${{ values.projectId }}-tfstate` |

Apply runs in the GitHub environment `production`, which is created on first use. Add required reviewers to it under **Settings > Environments** if an apply should wait for approval.
{%- if authType == 'jwt' or authType == 'oauth2' %}

### 5. Set the authentication variables

{% if authType == 'jwt' %}`jwt_issuer`, `jwt_jwks_uri` and `jwt_audiences` have{% else %}`oauth_client_ids` has{% endif %} no default. Set {% if authType == 'jwt' %}them{% else %}it{% endif %} in `infra/terraform.tfvars` as described under [Authentication](configuration.md#authentication); the Plan job fails until then.
{%- endif %}

The next push to `main`, or a manual run of the Deploy workflow, deploys the API.

## Run Terraform locally

Requires Terraform 1.9 or later{% if grpc %}, `protoc`{% endif %} and `gcloud auth application-default login`.

```bash
{%- if grpc %}
${{ protoc }}
{%- endif %}
cd infra
terraform init -backend-config="bucket=${{ values.projectId }}-tfstate" -backend-config="prefix=${{ values.destination.repo }}"
terraform plan
```
{%- if grpc %}

`infra/api_descriptor.pb` is the compiled form of `${{ values.grpcSpec }}` that Endpoints needs. It is a build product and is not committed: compile it again whenever the proto changes. `terraform validate` works without it; `terraform plan` stops with a message if it is missing.
{%- endif %}

The checks of the CI workflow can be run the same way: `terraform fmt -check -recursive`, `terraform init -backend=false && terraform validate && terraform test`, `tflint --init && tflint` and `trivy config --severity HIGH,CRITICAL infra`.

`terraform test` runs `infra/tests/configuration.tftest.hcl`: it plans the configuration with the values of `terraform.tfvars` against a mocked Google provider, without credentials, so a value that Terraform would reject at plan time fails the pull request.

## Call the API

```bash
GATEWAY=$(terraform -chdir=infra output -raw gateway_url)
{%- if not grpc or transcoding %}

curl "$GATEWAY/healthz"
curl{% if apiKey %} -H "x-api-key: $API_KEY"{% endif %}{% if tokenAuth %} -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN"{% endif %} "$GATEWAY/v1/items"
{%- endif %}
{%- if grpc %}

# gRPC, with https://github.com/fullstorydev/grpcurl
grpcurl -proto ${{ values.grpcSpec }} "${GATEWAY#https://}:443" ${{ package }}.Health/Check
grpcurl -proto ${{ values.grpcSpec }}{% if apiKey %} -H "x-api-key: $API_KEY"{% endif %}{% if tokenAuth %} -H "Authorization: Bearer $TOKEN"{% endif %} \
  "${GATEWAY#https://}:443" ${{ package }}.Items/ListItems
{%- endif %}
```
{%- if apiKey %}

`API_KEY` is a Google Cloud API key of a project that has enabled the API; see [Configuration](configuration.md).
{%- endif %}

A new gateway instance needs a few seconds to download the service configuration, so the first request after a period without traffic is slower. Set `gateway_min_instances` to `1` to keep an instance running.

## Remove the API

`terraform destroy` removes everything except two things:

- The gateway. The shared Cloud Run module leaves Cloud Run's deletion protection on, so Terraform refuses to delete the service. Delete it first with `gcloud run services delete ${{ values.apiName }}-gateway --project ${{ values.projectId }} --region europe-west1`, then run `terraform state rm 'module.gateway.google_cloud_run_v2_service.this'` and `terraform destroy`.
- The name of the Endpoints service. A deleted service can be restored for 30 days and its name cannot be reused during that time.
